==Legacy==

The 1982 AFA World Cup is widely regarded as a watershed moment for both Okaiken and international football. The tournament showcased Okaiken's rapid modernization to a global audience, occurring at the dawn of the [[Gogo 8090]] era—the period of unprecedented economic growth that would transform the nation into one of Adonia's largest economies.

Prime Minister [[Naoto Emi]], who took office just five months before the tournament, used the World Cup to announce Okaiken's "opening to the world" policy, which included the normalization of diplomatic relations with [[Zong]] later that year, which ended many years of conflict following the Great Adonian War. The tournament's success bolstered the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]]'s popularity and contributed to Emi's electoral victory in the [[1982 Okaiken general election|November 1982 general election]].

The tournament also marked a turning point in AFA World Cup history, introducing the expanded 36-team format that would be used until [[1994 AFA World Cup|1994]]. The innovative two-group-stage format, while controversial among some pundits, produced memorable matches and allowed for greater representation from emerging football nations.

For Balisca, the victory announced the nation's arrival as a footballing power. [[Matías Varela]]'s performances earned him a transfer to the Volisanian club [[AC Milanese]], where he would win three league titles and establish himself as one of the greatest players of his generation.

The infrastructure investments made for the tournament—particularly the construction of Tenji Park Olympic Stadium and the expansion of regional airports—contributed to Okaiken's tourism industry for decades afterward. Twelve of the tournament's venues continue to host professional football matches as of 2026.

